Actually, the original games (Gauntlet and Gauntlet II) required a
fair amount more thought.  Those games were about taking out
generators while exposing yourself to a minimum of harm, and were all
about finding good shot angles, knowing when to run headlong for the
exit and knowing when to sit still and overpower the monsters, keeping
generators you weren't ready for scrolled off the screen, and doing it
all quickly because your health was draining away second by inexorable
second.

Overall, they're much more interesting games than Legends and Dark
Legacy, which despite having much better graphics and character
advancement, a multitude of powerups, and actual endings (G and G II
were endurance games), they involve much less strategy.

Further, the home versions (ALL of them) took out the timed health
loss that made the arcade versions a race against time.  Playing one
of the more recent Gauntlets for a home system lets you take your
time, while the arcade games were frantic because every wasted second
carried you closer to the end of your credit.

